.TH "TONES" "1" "November 22, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
tones \- detect fire/EMS paging tones on radiod streams, record and announce them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tones
.RI "[options] group ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
tones listens to radiod audio streams (Opus, or PCM: 16-bit or float, any
rate) on the multicast groups
given, and finds two-tone sequential pages (a tone A, then a tone B, as fire
and EMS dispatch use to alert a department) and long tones. Each page is
written to standard output and to a CSV log, named by the department whose
tones it matches; pages that match none are logged as "unknown", with their
tones, which is how a department's tones can be found.
.P
Each page is recorded, from half a second before its first tone until the
channel has been quiet for a few seconds (radiod sends nothing while a
channel is squelched), as 8 kHz WAV (or Ogg Opus, with \-F ogg) named by
its time, department and frequency, eg 20261122T120314_Alden_453.500.wav. Further pages in the same
transmission go in the same recording. Once the recording is complete, the
command is run if any of its pages is a department in the notify list, with
the page in its environment: PAGE_DEPARTMENTS, PAGE_TONES, PAGE_TIME,
PAGE_FREQ, PAGE_FILE.
.P
A tone is one steady peak with most of the audio's power between 270 and
3400 Hz, above CTCSS and DCS; tones of at least 0.4 s, the second soon after
the first, make a page: a silence between them of up to 300 ms always, of
450 ms or more never (between, it depends on where it falls among the
detector's 50 ms looks). A tone of 4 s or more alone is a long tone. Tones
match a department within 1.5%. When a stream goes quiet (squelch), the
tones heard end there: a page cut off at the end of its tone B is still
found, and the next transmission's tones aren't joined to it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-t file, \-\-departments file"
The departments: lines of name, tone A and tone B in Hz, separated by commas
or tabs (a table pasted from a spreadsheet or web page does); tone B empty or
0 for a long tone. Lines without numbers, such as a header, are skipped.
.TP
.B "\-d dir, \-\-pages dir"
Where the recordings go (made if need be). Default ./pages.
.TP
.B "\-l file, \-\-log file"
The CSV log. Default pages.csv in the recordings' directory.
.TP
.B "\-c command, \-\-command command"
Run (by /bin/sh) once a page's recording is complete, for the departments
in \-n.
.TP
.B "\-n names, \-\-notify names"
The departments to run the command for, separated by commas, or * for all.
.TP
.B "\-q seconds, \-\-quiet seconds"
How long the channel must be quiet to end a recording. Default 5.
.TP
.B "\-m seconds, \-\-max seconds"
The longest recording. Default 120.
.TP
.B "\-T fraction, \-\-tolerance fraction"
How close a tone must be to a department's. Default 0.015.
.TP
.B "\-F wav|ogg, \-\-format wav|ogg"
The recordings' format: WAV (16-bit, 8 kHz; the default) or Ogg Opus (16
kb/s, about an eighth the size; .ogg).
.TP
.B "\-v, \-\-verbose"
Say which streams are heard.
.SH EXAMPLES
Alden's pages announced on a phone through ntfy, with the recording, and
every page logged and recorded:
.PP
.nf
tones \-t departments.csv \-d ~/pages \-n Alden \\
  \-c "NTFY_TOPIC=my\-long\-secret\-topic ~/ka9q\-radio/contrib/page\-alert\-ntfy.sh" \\
  fire\-dispatch.local
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
radiod(8), monitor(1), pcmrecord(1)
